<h2>Why the "just add bacteria" model fails</h2>
<p>
  Your gut is an ecosystem, not a vending machine. Bacteria are not something you insert — they are something
  you cultivate. Whether a probiotic strain survives and establishes depends on the environment it lands in:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Digestion has to be working.</strong> If food is arriving half-digested, the environment favors the wrong microbes — the fermenters that cause gas and bloating</li>
  <li><strong>The gut lining has to be intact.</strong> Good bacteria anchor to a healthy lining. An inflamed lining is hostile territory</li>
  <li><strong>What does not belong has to go first.</strong> Unwanted microbes out-compete newcomers; adding more soldiers does not win a battle that starts with the wrong army holding the fort</li>
</ul>
<p>
  That is why the model of "take this one bottle and feel better" produces so many disappointed customers. The
  bottle was fine. The order was wrong.
</p>

<h2>The order that actually works</h2>
<p>
  EmpowerX3's 90-Day Gut Rebuild is built around the sequence the gut itself demands:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Days 1–30 · Remove & Defend</strong> — restore digestion, clear out what does not belong and calm the gut</li>
  <li><strong>Days 31–60 · Repair & Repopulate</strong> — rebuild the gut lining, <em>then</em> bring in the good bacteria behind steady energy, better mood and deeper sleep</li>
  <li><strong>Days 61–90 · Release & Rebalance</strong> — a gentle detox phase, then foods come back one at a time so you learn exactly what works for your body</li>
</ol>
<p>
  Notice where probiotics sit: phase two, after the foundation exists. That is the difference between
  "taking probiotics" and "rebuilding a gut."
</p>

